% Urological Supplies: LCD L33803 and its Policy Article A52521 (DME MACs).
% A policy program of the DMEPOS model: it includes dmepos.le (orders,
% delivery, benefit category, continued need, refills) and states when a
% claim for a urinary catheter, an external urinary collection device or a
% supply used with them is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY:
%   indwelling catheters (A4311, A4312, A4313, A4314, A4315, A4316, A4338,
%   A4340, A4344, A4346) — one a month for routine maintenance, more for a
%   non-routine change the record documents; the specialty and all-silicone
%   catheters (A4340, A4344, A4312, A4315) with the documented need for
%   that catheter; the three-way catheters (A4346, A4313, A4316) only when
%   continuous irrigation is reasonable and necessary (a history of
%   obstruction, patency not maintained by intermittent irrigation);
%   intermittent catheterization — a catheter with lubricant (A4351, A4352,
%   A4332) or a hydrophilic catheter (A4295, A4296) when the beneficiary or
%   a caregiver can perform the procedure, the Coude tip (A4296, A4352) with
%   its documented need, the sterile kits (A4297, A4353) for one of the
%   criteria 1-4 (a nursing facility, immunosuppression, vesicoureteral
%   reflux, two urinary tract infections in the twelve months before the
%   kits), 200 a month;
%   external urinary collection devices (the male external catheter A4349,
%   35 a month; the specialty male external catheter A4326 with its
%   documented need; the female meatal cup A4327, pouch A4328 and Tibbe
%   device A4318) for urinary incontinence, never beside an indwelling
%   catheter;
%   the urethral insert (A4336) for an adult female with stress
%   incontinence, a diagnosis of the Article's ICD-10 list
%   (sources/icd10/A52521_icd10_covered.csv, one row, group 1: N39.3,
%   serving A4336 only; loaded as the table uro_icd10), without the
%   contraindications the LCD lists;
%   the supplies used with a covered catheter or device — the drainage bags
%   and bottle (A4357, A4358, A5102, A5105, A5112, the tray A4354), no leg
%   bag for a bedridden beneficiary and not both a vinyl and a latex leg
%   bag; the tray (A4310), tape (A4450, A4452) and anchoring devices (A4333,
%   A4334) of an indwelling catheter; the continuous irrigation tubing
%   (A4355) of a three-way catheter; the intermittent irrigation tray and
%   syringe (A4320, A4322) for an acute obstruction; each within the usual
%   maximum quantity of the LCD.
% The therapeutic irrigant A4321 is in scope and never covered ("will be
% denied as not reasonable and necessary"): no rule concludes it. The
% benefit is the Prosthetic Device benefit (Policy Article A52521, Social
% Security Act §1861(s)(8)), which carries no condition on the place of use.
%
% The payment conditions the Article adds (its "statutory payment policy
% requirements", section remedy): a permanent impairment of urination —
% urinary incontinence or urinary retention the record documents, of long
% and indefinite duration — and the dispensing cap of the LCD's refill
% paragraph (three months at a time). Once that benefit is met, continued
% need needs no further documentation (the Article's "CONTINUED MEDICAL
% NEED": an addition to the shared rules of dmepos.le).
%
% How the criteria are read. What the record states is a scenario fact: the
% documented conditions (the shared template "the medical record of B
% documents C": urinary incontinence, urinary retention, the indications of
% a non-routine change, of a specialty catheter, of immunosuppression, the
% contraindications of the urethral insert — each from a list below with
% its passage), whether the beneficiary or a caregiver can perform the
% procedure, the reflux, the number of urinary tract infections (a record
% of an infection stands for one meeting the LCD's definition: a culture
% above 10,000 colony forming units with a sign or symptom of the list),
% being bedridden, the residence, sex and age (adult: 18 years). Supplies
% used with a catheter or device are its accessories (the shared
% template). The quantities are the LCD's, as data (the tables
% uro_quantity and uro_period, the period in days: a month is read as 31
% days, the longest, so that one a day is 31 a month; a week as 7, three
% months as 93), read against the quantity billed and the months the claim
% supplies. For the tape, the anchoring devices,
% the sterile kits, the female devices and the irrigation tubing the LCD
% denies any quantity above the maximum; for the others a greater quantity
% is covered when the record documents a non-routine change (the catheters
% and drainage supplies) or explains it (the finding). The findings this
% policy reads, and the criterion each stands for:
%   permanent impairment of urination (the Article: "If the medical record,
%   including the judgment of the treating practitioner, indicates the
%   condition is of long and indefinite duration, the test of permanence is
%   considered met");
%   excess urological supplies explained ("The medical necessity for use of
%   a greater quantity of supplies than the amounts specified in the policy
%   must be well documented"; consulted only when the maximum is exceeded
%   and no non-routine change is documented);
%   specialty catheter necessary (the specialty or all-silicone catheter,
%   when none of the LCD's examples is documented: "not all-inclusive");
%   intermittent irrigation insufficient (continuous irrigation: "the
%   patency of the catheter cannot be maintained by intermittent irrigation
%   in conjunction with reasonable and necessary catheter changes");
%   coude tip catheter necessary (when the inability to catheterize with a
%   straight tip catheter is not documented);
%   specialty external catheter necessary (A4326: "covered only when
%   documentation substantiates the medical necessity for such a catheter").
%
% Not modelled: the inFlow device (A4341, A4342: permanent urinary
% retention due to impaired detrusor contractility, once every 29 days, the
% re-evaluation between the 31st and 91st day); the insertion tray "per
% episode" and the combined utilization of catheters and bags (a count of
% episodes no claim states); the combined 200 kits of A4297 and A4353 (each
% claim is read alone); continuous irrigation for more than 2 weeks; the
% appliance cleaner A5131, the external urethral clamp A4356, the extension
% tubing A4331, the leg straps A5113 and A5114, the anchoring device A5200;
% the sterile water or saline A4217 (also a nebulizer code); the AU, KX,
% GA, GY and GZ modifiers, the Column I / Column II bundling, the supplies
% used in a practitioner's office, and the incontinence products the
% Article denies as not prosthetic (A4520, A4553, A4554, A6250, A9270).
% The codes A4357 and A5102 are ostomy supplies too, and A4450 and A4452
% are ostomy supplies and surgical dressings as well: in a model holding
% those policies such a claim meets them all.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % the record
    *a beneficiary* or a caregiver can perform the procedure; undefined.
    vesicoureteral reflux of *a beneficiary* while on intermittent catheterization is radiologically documented; undefined.
    *a number* urinary tract infections on intermittent catheterization in the twelve months before the sterile kits are documented for *a beneficiary*; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* is bedridden; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* is female; undefined.
    the age of *a beneficiary* is *a number* years; undefined.
    % the tables
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table uro_icd10.
    the usual maximum for urological supply *a code* is *a quantity* under table uro_quantity.
    the period of the usual maximum for urological supply *a code* is *a number* days under table uro_period.
    % derived
    *an item* is an indwelling catheter.
    *a device* meets the urological coverage criteria.
    *a condition* is a nonroutine catheter change indication.
    *a condition* is a specialty catheter indication.
    the need for a specialty catheter is documented for *a beneficiary*.
    continuous irrigation is reasonable and necessary for *a beneficiary*.
    the need for a coude tip catheter is documented for *a beneficiary*.
    *a beneficiary* meets a sterile intermittent catheter kit criterion.
    *a condition* is an immunosuppressing condition for sterile kits.
    *a beneficiary* uses an indwelling catheter.
    the diagnosis code of *a beneficiary* is a covered urethral insert diagnosis code.
    *a condition* is a contraindication to urethral inserts.
    *a beneficiary* has a contraindication to urethral inserts.
    *an item* is an excluded urinary drainage supply.
    *a claim* is within the usual maximum quantity of urological supplies.
    *a claim* is within the listed amount of urological supplies.
    the urological supply quantity of *a claim* is allowed for *a beneficiary*.
    *a claim* is a documented nonroutine change for *a beneficiary*.
    the excess quantity of *a claim* may be explained.
    *a code* has a strict urological maximum.
    *a beneficiary* has a permanent impairment of urination.
    the impairment of urination of *a beneficiary* is documented.
    *a claim* is within the dispensing limit for urological supplies.

% The Policy Article's ICD-10 table: female stress incontinence, the one
% code of group 1, serving the urethral insert A4336.
the table uro_icd10 is loaded from sources/icd10/A52521_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

